\caption(The schematic arrows show the first capped outward move. The second raw step slightly raises the regularized potential, so a temperature cap alone is not a descent guarantee.)
\alttext(A native two-node undirected diagram labels the initial half-unit separation, repulsion one, attraction about0.353553 and vertex-zero first capped movement minus0.1.)

> The force law and its regularization

For positions p_u and p_v, write Delta=p_u−p_v, raw distance d=||Delta||,
and h=max(delta,d) with delta=1e−9. The implementation adds Delta*k²/h² to u
and subtracts it from v for repulsion. Each nonloop edge record instead adds
−Delta*h/k to u and the opposite to v. Above the floor these give the familiar
magnitudes k²/d and d²/k. Below it, both forces shrink linearly to zero at
coincidence; the code does not select a random direction for identical points.

If F_u is the accumulated vector, the actual movement is

$$
p_u\leftarrow p_u+\frac{F_u}{s_u}\min(s_u,t_i),\qquad
s_u=\max(10^{-9},\|F_u\|),\qquad
t_i=0.1\left(1-\frac{\operatorname{double}(i)}{T}\right),\quad i=0,\ldots,T-1.
$$
\alttext(All forces are accumulated first. A vertex then moves along its displacement, using a distance floor and a linear temperature cap.)

The distance floor also applies to the movement denominator. Thus the exact rule
near zero is the expression above, rather than an unrestricted normalized direction.
For practical representable schedules the last temperature is approximately0.1/T;
very large integers can round when converted to double. T=0 performs no division by
T because there are no sweeps. No velocity, box confinement, stopping tolerance,
line search or Barnes–Hut approximation is implemented.

The historical model and direct algorithm are described in Fruchterman and Reingold,
[Graph Drawing by Force-directed Placement](https://www.reingold.co/force-directed.pdf),
Software—Practice and Experience21(11),1129–1164(1991),
[doi:10.1002/spe.4380211102](https://doi.org/10.1002/spe.4380211102).
This library's variant omits the paper's frame clamping and random coincident-point
directions. Its distance floor, seeded initializer, fixed schedule and final scaling
are concrete implementation choices, rather than a claim to reproduce every paper variant.

> The original seeded four-vertex path

The original example draws0--1--2--3 with500 sweeps and seed7. The first four
output lines retain the current canonical function's original coordinates. A local
mt19937_64 draws two uniform variates per point in [−0.5,0.5); the seed reproduces
these values within the same standard-library environment. Different C++ library
implementations need not map a uniform distribution to identical coordinate bits.
The figure uses undirected paths, so its links carry no arrowheads.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/force_directed_original.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/force_directed_layout.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto edges=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_example_edges();const auto p=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout(4,edges,500,7);
  for(const auto&v:p)std::cout<<v.x<<' '<<v.y<<'\n';
  const auto w=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_work(4,edges,500,7).work;const auto m=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_metrics(p,edges);
  std::print("\nOriginal work: {} pair visits; {} attraction visits; {} moves; {} uniform variates.\n",w.pair_visits,w.attraction_visits,w.vertex_moves,w.uniform_values);
  std::print("Final geometry: centroid ({:.6f}, {:.6f}); maximum centered radius {:.6f}; mean edge length {:.6f}.\n",double(m.centroid_x),double(m.centroid_y),double(m.max_radius),double(m.mean_edge_length));
  dm::graphics::Diagram d(130,900);dm::graphics::DiagramStroke link;link.color={37,99,235};link.width=3;dm::graphics::DiagramNodeStyle node;node.circle=true;node.width=.65;node.height=.65;node.font_size=24;
  for(auto[u,v]:edges)d.path({{3*p[u].x,3*p[u].y},{3*p[v].x,3*p[v].y}},link);
  for(std::size_t u=0;u<p.size();++u)d.node(std::to_string(u),{3*p[u].x,3*p[u].y},std::to_string(u),node);
  d.text({0,3.6},"Original path: 500 sweeps, seed7",25).text({0,-3.6},"Centered and maximum-radius scaled output",23).save_svg("build/force_directed_original.svg");
  if(w.pair_visits!=3000||w.attraction_visits!=1500||w.vertex_moves!=2000)throw std::runtime_error("original work");
}
