\image(figures/force_directed_original.svg)
// result_end
\caption(The original path is centered and scaled after500 sweeps. Equal diagram scales on x and y preserve the geometry; its original actual coordinates are printed above.)
\alttext(A native undirected four-node path places vertices zero through three along the original descending diagonal, with zero at the upper left and three at the lower right.)

For n>=2 the final pass computes the double mean in vertex order, subtracts it,
finds the maximum centered radius r, then divides every point by r when r>0.
Consequently the displayed centroid is approximately zero and nondegenerate maximum
radius approximately one. These are floating-point properties, not exact certificates.
The unit-area force parameter k does not impose a square boundary on raw positions.
For two vertices joined by one edge, the unregularized force equilibrium separation
is k; final radius normalization instead makes their output separation two. Raw
and final distances must therefore be interpreted in different units.

> Inspect one actual run, rather than rerunning prefixes

The owned trace captures initial coordinates, every sweep's repulsion and net
accumulators, positions after all moves, raw final coordinates, final coordinates
and actual normalization metadata. It uses the same kernel without replay.
Running an independent prefix with length t would change the cooling denominator;
it would not reproduce the first t sweeps of the T-sweep run.

A useful diagnostic integrates this implementation's regularized forces. Let
U=the sum of U_r over unordered pairs plus U_a over nonloop edge records, where

$$
U_r(d)=\begin{cases}-k^2\log d,&d\ge\delta,\\
-k^2\log\delta+\frac{k^2}{2}(1-(d/\delta)^2),&d<\delta,\end{cases}\qquad
U_a(d)=\begin{cases}d^3/(3k),&d\ge\delta,\\
\delta d^2/(2k)-\delta^3/(6k),&d<\delta.\end{cases}
$$
\alttext(The repulsion and attraction potentials are continuous with continuous first derivatives at the distance floor and differentiate to the library's underlying regularized vector forces.)

The joins agree in value and derivative at delta. Differentiation gives the
regularized forces with the negative-gradient sign; zero separation has zero vector
force. This is our diagnostic derivation for the library's law. Actual arithmetic
is rounded, and the movement cap does not ensure that U decreases each sweep.
Long-double metrics are queried separately at O(n squared+m) cost per frame; those
queries are not included in the actual kernel counters. The next potential curve
uses only raw states, before final normalization changes the fixed-k potential.

```{cpp, results="markdown", file="figures/force_directed_history.svg", flags="-std=c++23 -O2 $(pkg-config --cflags --libs datamunge) -Wl,-rpath,$(pkg-config --variable=libdir datamunge)"}
#include <dm/dm.hpp>
#include <datamunge/algorithms/force_directed_layout.hpp>
#include <datamunge/plot/diagram.hpp>
#include <print>
#include <iostream>
#include <limits>
namespace dm=datamunge;
int main(){
  std::filesystem::create_directories("build");
  const auto edges=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_example_edges();const auto t=dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_trace(4,edges,500,7);std::vector<double> x,potential,step_x,temperature,movement;
  x.push_back(0);potential.push_back(double(dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_metrics(t.initial,edges).potential));auto previous=t.initial;std::size_t rises=0;double largest_move=0;
  for(const auto&f:t.frames){const auto value=double(dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_metrics(f.positions,edges).potential);rises+=value>potential.back();double moved=0;for(std::size_t u=0;u<previous.size();++u)moved=std::max(moved,std::hypot(f.positions[u].x-previous[u].x,f.positions[u].y-previous[u].y));largest_move=std::max(largest_move,moved);x.push_back(double(f.iteration+1));potential.push_back(value);step_x.push_back(double(f.iteration+1));temperature.push_back(f.temperature);movement.push_back(moved);previous=f.positions;}
  std::print("Actual history: {} frames; {} potential increases; largest raw movement {:.6f}; last temperature {:.6f}.\n",t.frames.size(),rises,largest_move,t.frames.back().temperature);
  std::print("Raw initial potential {:.6f}; raw final potential {:.6f}; normalized output potential {:.6f}.\n",potential.front(),potential.back(),double(dm::algorithms::force_directed_layout_metrics(t.positions,edges).potential));
  std::print("Normalization metadata: mean ({:.6f}, {:.6f}); raw scale {:.6f}.\n",t.work.mean.x,t.work.mean.y,t.work.scale);
  auto energy=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();energy.lines(x,potential,"Raw potential",{37,99,235},2).title("Raw regularized potential").xlab("Completed sweeps; zero is initialization").ylab("Diagnostic U in raw coordinate units");
  auto motion=dm::graphics::RPlot::create();motion.lines(step_x,temperature,"Temperature",{220,38,38},2).lines(step_x,movement,"Largest move",{37,99,235},1.5).legend({"Temperature","Largest move"},{{220,38,38},{37,99,235}}).title("Cap and actual largest move").xlab("Completed sweep").ylab("Raw distance");
  dm::graphics::RLayout::create(1,2).add(energy).add(motion).size(1100,520).title("One executing 500-sweep run; metrics queried afterward").fit_panels().save_svg("build/force_directed_history.svg");
  if(t.frames.size()!=500||largest_move>.10000000001)throw std::runtime_error("actual history bounds");
}
