\image(figures/force_directed_work.svg)
// result_end
\caption(Ten-sweep complete graphs expose quadratic pair visits and linear vertex moves. Attraction visits equal pair visits for these particular complete graphs; the table retains both counts.)
\alttext(A native line plot compares actual repulsion pair visits and vertex moves for ten sweeps with two, four, eight, sixteen and thirty-two vertices on a linear count scale.)

Every endpoint must lie in0..n−1, and every supplied coordinate must be finite.
Full domain validation precedes quota checks or copies, including early-return cases.
All actual double differences, accumulators, distances, updates and normalization
must remain finite. Finite input alone does not guarantee finite intermediate
arithmetic: the attraction of a very distant pair can overflow. The same extreme
pair without an edge is accepted when its actual repulsion and normalization remain
finite; unused attractive forces are not computed. Failures return no partial result
and never mutate supplied positions or edges.

Let S=T for n>=2 and S=0 otherwise. Complete trace admission checks

$$
C=E+3n+S(1+3n).
$$
\alttext(The owned trace counts edge records, three point arrays and one frame plus three point arrays per actual sweep.)

C counts entry records, not bytes or peak RAM. It includes edges, initial/raw/final
point arrays and every frame's positions/repulsion/net vectors; temporary kernel
scratch is additional. The default cap is one million entries and zero disables
only the optional cap; arithmetic and container capacity checks remain. Admission
happens before RNG or input copying and captures every sweep without truncation.
A supplied-position call starts a fresh cooling schedule, not an exact continuation
of a previous schedule. Histories cost O(E+n+S*n) owned entry storage, while optional
per-frame diagnostic queries add their own quadratic work.

The library retains the original seeded path behavior and the ordinary graph-layout
include. Source programs above regenerate all local figures with canonical C++ APIs.
The deterministic geometric controls require no external dataset.
